--- rust-1b.txt (registered)
+++ rust-1b-amended-bar.txt
@@ -56,0 +57,15 @@
+ READINGS AND RECORDS FOR THE STAGE 2 RUNNER (amendment, before start). The registered text above is kept whole: nothing is deleted, the amendment adds the paragraphs from here to the SENTENCES paragraph. Each item is a reading of a rule that this bar leaves open, or a record that the freeze file carries. The literal host-load sentence of this bar (HYGIENE and INVALID, "uptime load average below 0.3 and fx status clear before and after") is read by R1 as rust-1 reads it in its amended bar (bar_sha256 a4f1a43eadcd8a9ab2e62c0e0e924d6fba865944418ae337a8bc68513dec6467, items R1 to R9); R2, R5, R7, R8 and R10 carry the items R2, R5, R6, R7 and R9 of that amendment over, and R4 carries its loader correction over and states the loader of the stage 2 arms as their own code does it. Five items change how a rule of this bar is applied and are named so that nobody reads the amendment as additions that leave the rules alone: R1 changes when the load rule (an INVALID rule and a HYGIENE rule) is tested and adds a void rule during the run, with limits relative to an idle baseline measured in the job; R2 changes how the clock-drop rule (HYGIENE and INVALID) is measured and what it is compared with; R3 reads "a harness bug in one arm" as a failed untimed check of that arm and adds NOT RUN for an arm that cannot run on a host; R9 changes how the rule "arm order rotated each rep" is applied (balanced designs of 5 orders in place of a one-step rotation); R11 runs the box1 work as two jobs in a fixed order, which narrows what can be claimed and makes no label easier. R3 and R12 add what the result must state about arm Q and about the scope of the KERNELS sentence. None of the twelve items moves a boundary, a band, a statistic, a pair definition, a host role, a cap, an expected value or a label.
+ R1 Load. "uptime load average below 0.3 and fx status clear before and after" is read as: at the start of every arm run the 1-minute load average is below 0.3 and no other leg-* or sv5-* user unit is active and no llama-bench or ggml-rpc-server process is alive (the runner waits up to 10 minutes; if the host does not settle the rep is void and repeated); at the end of the run no other such unit is active; the load average at the end is recorded in every row and is not gated, because one single-thread arm run leaves the 1-minute load average near 1.0, so a gate on it would void every rep. In place of an idle test after the run, the runner measures the other load during the run itself: it reads the busy CPU time per logical CPU from /proc/stat (user, nice, system, irq, softirq and steal, in seconds) before the arm process starts and after it ends, and subtracts the arm process's own CPU time (utime plus stime of the reaped child) and the runner's own process CPU time. The two figures of a rep are (a) that remainder on the arm's CPU or CPUs and on their hyper-threading sibling(s), together, as a fraction of the arm process's wall time, and (b) the same remainder over all logical CPUs of the host, in CPUs averaged over the arm process's wall time. The void limits are relative to an idle baseline measured in the job. After the idle gate has passed and before the opening rep (nothing timed yet, no arm running), the runner measures the baseline with the same foreign-load formula it uses for a rep: 6 windows of 20 s each. The baseline is the maximum over the 6 windows, separately for (a) and for (b). The limits are the baseline plus a margin, 2 percentage points for (a) and 0.05 CPU for (b), and never below 2 percent for (a) and 0.10 CPU for (b). A rep is void, recorded as VOID and repeated, when its figure (a) is above limit (a) or its figure (b) is above limit (b). If the baseline itself is above 15 percent for (a) or above 0.30 CPU for (b), the job ends INVALID with the reason host_not_idle and nothing is timed. The baseline (all 6 windows and the two maxima), the margins, the floors and the resulting limits are printed in the RESULT line and are carried in every row, next to the two figures of the rep; the margins, the floors and the baseline rule are fixed by this amendment, and no rep is excluded after the fact for any other load. Voids from this rule and from the idle test are counted together; more than 6 voids in one job stop the job as PARTIAL.
+ R2 Clock. "CPU MHz from /proc/cpuinfo before and after each rep, a rep with more than 15 percent drop is discarded" is read as: the reading is the MHz of the CPU the arm is pinned to (R7; at N=2 the opening one of the two arm CPUs), taken from /proc/cpuinfo once a second from 2 s after the start of the arm process to its end. The reference is the median of the per-rep medians of the kept reps of that host so far (all arms and both phases); for the opening rep of a host it is that rep's own median. A rep is discarded and repeated when its lowest reading or its median is more than 15 percent below the reference, or when a thermal-throttle counter of the host changed during it (counters exist where the kernel exposes thermal_throttle in sysfs; the clock rule alone applies elsewhere). The third discard on a host makes the host INVALID, as the bar says. The reference is not the highest MHz seen anywhere in the job: on hosts with the schedutil governor and turbo (box2, box3) idle readings change by more than 15 percent within seconds, so such a reference would void the host by construction.
+ R3 Checks of every arm. The bar asks for the same weights, prompts, token ids and thread counts in both arms of a pair and names "a harness bug in one arm" as INVALID. This is read as an untimed check of every arm that runs on a host, before the timed reps and again after them, in the same process form as the timed run (--plain check over the 9 work rows: the 8 timed prompts and the warm-up row). The check must end with exit code 0 and 9 CHECK rows, and the arg-max must equal the forced id at every one of the 64 positions of every row (mismatches 0); the Q8_0 extra is exempt from the arg-max condition because its arithmetic differs by design, and its mismatch count is recorded. The largest absolute difference between the arm's logits and the L-C logits (file ref_logits.f32, produced by the strict-order build of cis2_ref whose witnesses equal REF_WITNESS.txt; its sha256 is in the freeze record) is recorded for G-OB, G-MKL, X, T and Q (the bar names it for G-OB; the others are extra, reported, no label). The witness of the second check (sha256 over the logits bytes and the arg-max of every position) must equal that of the before-check. A failed check makes that arm INVALID on that host, which gives no label to a pair that holds it; the other arms go on. An arm that cannot run on a host (killed by a signal such as SIGILL, a missing library or loader, a failed install of the Python stack of T from the shipped wheelhouse) is recorded NOT RUN with the reason; this is not a job failure, and a pair that holds it gets no label on that host. Arm Q is not bit-comparable arithmetic: in the untimed gate run on penguin (checks of the arms without any timing) its largest difference to the L-C logits was 0.072, against 0.0002 for each of the other arms, with the arg-max equal to the forced id at all 64 positions. The result therefore reports the largest logit difference of Q next to every Q speed line, together with the INFO line that the Q driver prints (system_info and flash_attn_type) for the host, and does not describe Q as bit-comparable arithmetic or as the same arithmetic as G, X or T. The check run of T also records the hidden-state hash of TRUST COLUMN item 2. The time of the after-check is reserved inside the cap test (R8).
+ R4 Builds, references and formats. Each Rust arm (G-OB, G-MKL, X) is built at opt-level 2 and at opt-level 3 (lto fat, codegen-units 1, panic abort, RUSTFLAGS unset, target x86-64 baseline gnu); both builds run as separate arms in every rep, and the reference of the arm on a host is the build with the higher median over its kept reps, applied mechanically to the medians as the bar says; Band W uses that reference. Arm Q is built from the pinned llama.cpp commit once per ISA class, with the ISA flags fixed explicitly so that the binary hashes are frozen before the initial timing: avx2 (SSE4.2, AVX, AVX2, BMI2, FMA, F16C) for box1 and sse42 (no AVX) for box2 and box3, which is the reading of "built with the host's own flags"; the runner picks the build by the avx2 flag of the host CPU. The weights of G, X, T and Q are the fp32 conversion of the pinned bf16 values (an exact shift, file shipped in the payload, sha256 in the freeze record), and Q reads a GGUF F32 and the Q8_0 extra a GGUF Q8_0, both made from that fp32 file by the convert_hf_to_gguf.py of the pinned commit; each host verifies the sha256 of its own pinned bf16 file (sv5/arma/weights) and recomputes the conversion hash from it. The KV cache of Q is F32 (set explicitly), so that "F32" holds for every tensor. The load method of each arm is stated in the result as its own code does it (G and X: the arm's own loader parses the safetensors header and reads each tensor with read_exact into its own heap buffer, with no whole-file buffer and no mmap, which differs from the std::fs::read of the whole file in cis2_ref that rust-1 found; T: safetensors load_file; Q: llama.cpp default, memory-mapped), and the peak PSS lines carry that I/O-method effect. The FRAMEWORK memory line is written in this form: "FRAMEWORK memory (no label, different load paths): peak PSS of G <x> MB, T <x> MB and Q <x> MB on <host>; L-R <x> MB and L-C <x> MB are rows of rust-1, taken earlier, on a different day and in a different job. G reads each tensor with read_exact into its own heap buffer (no whole-file buffer, no mmap); T loads with safetensors load_file; Q memory-maps the GGUF (llama.cpp default); L-R reads the whole file with std::fs::read into a heap buffer and L-C read()s it, as rust-1 records."
+ R5 CPU quota. The feeder sets CPUQuota to 100 percent times the number of logical CPUs on every job; cm-feed cannot leave the quota out. "no CPUQuota" is read as "no binding CPUQuota"; the runner logs cpu.max at every cgroup level, the result says whether a quota binds and states this reading as a disclosure.
+ R6 Records kept for the unlabelled lines. The Python stack of T is installed in the job from the shipped, hash-checked wheelhouse (offline, requirements.lock, torch 2.14.0+cpu), and its package list must equal the lock; the torch configuration, the MKL or OpenBLAS state, the dispatch line and the MKL_VERBOSE head of T and of G-MKL are kept from untimed probe runs that are not part of any rep; the OpenBLAS core type of G-OB is kept from an untimed probe. These probe runs print per-call figures of the library (MKL_VERBOSE); they are kept as the bar asks ("MKL_VERBOSE output kept") and are never used in a speed pair. Penguin gives no timing number and no per-call figure is kept from it: the checks on penguin give exit codes, witnesses, mismatch counts, logit differences and thread counts alone.
+ R7 Pinning. The arm process is pinned to one CPU and the runner (the 50 ms sampler) to a CPU of a different physical core when the host has two or more; at N=2 the arm is pinned to one CPU of each of two physical cores and the runner shares the core of the opening arm CPU (its sibling when the host has hyper-threading), its own CPU time being subtracted by the formula of R1. The result states the CPUs. The same pin applies to the untimed checks.
+ R8 Cap accounting. The caps are lab totals per host, shared with rust-1. Every job writes its elapsed seconds into a file on the host (the file that rust-1 uses); a later job on that host (including the single redo as 10 reps) gets the registered cap minus the sum of those, and never more. The cap test before each arm run counts the elapsed time, the longest run of that arm so far (or its check run before any timed run) and the reserved time of the after-check; an arm run that would not fit is not started and the arm is recorded COST-CAPPED and gets no label.
+ R9 Order. "arm order rotated each rep" is read as balanced designs of 5 orders (rep k uses the order of rep k mod 5), fixed now in the runner: phase n1 holds 9 arms (G-OB, G-MKL and X at both opt-levels, T, Q, Q8_0) and phase n2 holds 7 (the same without X, which is a single-thread engine). In every design every arm holds the same mean position over the 5 orders (4.0 of positions 0 to 8 in phase n1, 3.0 of positions 0 to 6 in phase n2), hence over the 10 reps of a redo (the orders repeat); no arm stands at the same position in two consecutive orders. A one-step rotation of 9 or 7 arms is balanced over 9 or 7 reps and not over 5 or 10. A discarded or void rep is repeated at the same position. The result states the position of every arm in every rep and the mean positions.
+ R10 Hosts and phases. (a) The timing ruling of 2026-10-04 00:40Z (state/BOXES.md) is the reading of the RULE A paragraph for this bar, as in rust-1: timing is allowed on an idle host with no binding CPUQuota and with load, MHz and throttle records per rep; box1 is the labelled host, box2 and box3 are shown with no label. (b) The box1 job wrapper pauses cm-tick.timer and cm-rc-watch.timer (the periodic user units that would overlap reps) for the duration of the job and restores them on every exit path; RESULT prints timers_paused= and timers_restored=. It does not stop the feeder's own units or the job unit, and it does not stop a service that is already running. (c) Phase n1 (N=1, labels on box1) runs on box1, box2 and box3; phase n2 (N=2, reported with no label) runs on box1 and box2 and is not part of the 10-rep redo; on box1 the two phases are two jobs in a fixed order (R11), on box2 one job holds both phases and on box3 one job holds phase n1. (d) box4 and box5 run no arm of this bar, as the bar says; the job wrappers exist for box1, box2 and box3 alone.
+ R11 Box1 jobs, order and budget. On box1 the registered 8 box-hours are shared with rust-1, whose jobs had used 6,735 s of the 28,800 s when this amendment was written (file ~/.rust1_budget_box1), so about 22,065 s remain. From the speeds in EXPECTED VALUES (an estimate, not a timing) one job that holds phase n1 and phase n2 with 5 reps takes about 4.1 h and the 10-rep redo of phase n1 about 3.7 h, and both do not fit in what remains. The box1 work is therefore two jobs: the N=1 job (phase n1, 5 reps, wrapper rust1b-box1-n1.sh) and the N=2 job (phase n2, 5 reps, wrapper rust1b-box1-n2.sh); the estimates are about 7,900 s for the N=1 job and about 7,200 s for the N=2 job (arithmetic in BUILD.md). The order is fixed before the start: the N=1 job runs before any N=2 work on box1; if this bar calls for the single 10-rep redo of phase n1 on box1 (a label INCONCLUSIVE), the redo runs before the N=2 job; the N=2 job runs after that, once the N=1 result has been read, if the budget left on box1 (28,800 s minus the sum of the box-seconds file) is at least its estimate of 7,200 s, which the wrapper of the N=2 job tests before anything runs; if the budget left is smaller, N=2 on box1 is reported COST-CAPPED and gets no number. This narrows what can be claimed (N=2 on box1 may be absent) and makes no label easier: no band, boundary, statistic, pair, host role, cap, expected value or label moves. Phase n1 on box1 carries every label of this bar, so it is never the phase that is cut.
+ R12 Scope of the KERNELS sentence. Arm X replaces the matrix-vector product (every linear layer and the output projection) and nothing else: attention (score dot products, softmax and the weighted sum of values), rope, rmsnorm, the activation and the residual additions stay on the strict scalar code of cis2_ref that arm G shares. The KERNELS sentence of this bar is written with one clause added after "This is a statement about our kernels.": "The engine replaces the matrix-vector product alone; attention, softmax and rope stay on the strict scalar path that G shares." The rest of the sentence template is unchanged, and "engine: NOT TESTED." is unchanged.
+ FREEZE FILE CONTENT (amendment, before start). The freeze record state/reports/rust-lab/rust-1b/FREEZE-STAGE2.md also holds the sha256 of the payload list PAYLOAD_SHA256 (which lists the sha256 of every binary, library, weights file, GGUF, wheel, input and of run.py), of the runner run.py and of every job wrapper, the lines of the decode loop of each arm (TRUST COLUMN item 4) and the dependency count of each arm (item 3). The wrappers carry the frozen payload and runner values; the wrapper and the runner compare them with the files on the host before anything runs and end the job INVALID on a difference, and every RESULT line prints the actual and the frozen values, so that a change after start is visible from the RESULT line alone. The start record is written after this amended bar is in the public ledger.
